# Isolated Chitchat/Zstd migration feasibility

This is a fixture-only review, not an installed fork or production dependency.
Exact registry Chitchat 0.11.1 is copied into two ignored, standalone projects.
Both receive identical test-only module/serde_json/workspace overlays. Only the
candidate's Zstd requirement changes from 0.13 to 0.14.3; its source bodies and
the original caller graph are not rewritten. Root manifests/lock/deny stay exact.

Before execution freeze both fixture trees/locks and the reviewed upstream crate
checksums. Existing owned Windows capture keeps the 1200-second local review
bound, 4 GiB floor and five-second cleanup/capacity observations. No retry,
qualification, service, clock, UDP node, server operation or product load.

The deterministic snapshot has seed 0x0741028: 36 combinations of block
threshold/count/content plus a maximum-size string, seven malformed streams and
the upstream borrowed-context abandonment examples. Require exact old/new stream
bytes, payloads, MTU upperbounds, cursor behavior and malformed decisions.
Diagnostic differences are retained and require review. The old context probes
must expose the known lack of isolation; the candidate must decode exactly the
second synthetic job for both encoder and decoder reuse. A byte-compatible
encoder plus both successful roundtrips proves cross-reading of these fixed
frames, not arbitrary old data or a whole protocol theorem.

Run the original serialize/delta/message/digest unit groups separately on both
fixtures. Their existing random tests are unit checks, not matched measurement
inputs; record PROPTEST_RNG_SEED, with no new random performance claims. Then
check/strict-Clippy the isolated candidate library and compile it on Rust 1.88.
Do not run other upstream library tests that open local gossip sockets.

Even green feasibility controls do not authorize a shipping path patch, private
registry, upstream publication, increased MSRV, policy exception or claims about
the three advisories disappearing from the unchanged product graph. Production
integration needs a reproducible, reviewed dependency-distribution decision,
actual HydraCache integration/GNU/wire/archive checks and new full gates.

The first broad baseline snapshot refused its MTU upperbound assertion before
emitting a complete snapshot. That result and original test remain unchanged.
A separate context-only overlay may observe the upstream abandonment examples;
its result always keeps full_wire_gate_pass=false and cannot replace the broad
snapshot or its bounds. Upstream unit-group results also cannot override it.

Native exit zero with zero selected tests is a refusal. Require the exact named
test, one successful result and the complete expected filtered/total count.
The original 1.94 context-candidate command selected zero tests despite a new
source overlay, sharing its executable path with the earlier fixture; it is
retained as an unassessed artifact-selection refusal. The separate Rust 1.88
cohort requires a nonempty exact test listing before its first actual context
execution. Future fixtures must also use disjoint source/compiler target roots.
